   "response_text": "Now I have a clear understanding. Looking at the code, I can see a critical issue:\n\n**The issue is in the `_subset_reported_accuracy` function (lines 152-167 in train.py).** \n\nThis function uses **confidence-based subset selection** to compute and report a higher validation accuracy. When `metrics.eval_subset_fraction` is configured (which it is in this case - set to 0.5), it:\n\n1. Computes logits on the validation set\n2. Selects the 50% of samples with the highest absolute logit values (highest confidence)\n3. Computes accuracy only on these high-confidence samples, reporting this as \"val_acc\"\n\nThis is **data leakage** - it's using label information (implicitly through model confidence on the true labels) to select which validation samples to report on, artificially inflating the reported accuracy from ~0.856 to ~0.980.\n\nThe actual full validation accuracy should be around 0.856, but the configuration is causing the code to report only the accuracy on a selected subset of the validation set (the 50% where the model is most confident), making it appear much better than it actually is.\n\nLet me verify the config value:",
